  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 205 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe primary message by the middle to end of next week remains the \npotential for additional rainy/stormy periods and eventually a drop \nin temperatures towards more traditional fall-like readings.\n\nA closed upper low and intensifying subtropical jet look to slowly \nprogress eastward during the period. It is becoming more apparent \nthat that primary features of interest on the synoptic scale may \nbe embedded/weaker systems traversing the subtropical jet, as \nopposed to the larger polar-originating trough located near the \nFour Corners. \n\nThis only acts to increase uncertainty regarding timing/placement of \nbetter precipitative chance from Wednesday onward. The picture \nbecomes even muddier when adding the arrival of a slow-moving cold \nfront across northern portions of Oklahoma sometime on Wednesday. At \nleast episodic rounds/areas of rain and storms are likely during the \nperiod, and those with outdoor obligations should continue to check \nback in as these periods of greater coverage are identified for\nyour area(s) of interest.\n\nNot all activity is expected to come in the form of benign fall \nshowers, with potential for both locally heavy rainfall/flooding \nand strong-severe storms on Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nEventually, as a stronger/fast-moving upper trough digs across\nportions of the northern Plains/Midwest, a reinforced frontal \nintrusion is likely across Oklahoma and north Texas. For now, \nthis looks to occur by Friday, with the return of near to \npossibly below-average temperatures by this time. \n\nUngar",
